# Rutile interface

## Implemented screens

- **Loading.** A one-second black-screen intro reveals the connected Joeltech JT monogram diagonally in Joeltech blue before fading it out. The lab-grown rutile then spins and zooms in, with prismatic rays around the crystal and the white Rutile title appearing over it. There is no Skip intro button; reduced motion bypasses the intro.
- **Title.** Use **Start** or press a character key, Enter, or Space to open the main menu. The gem responds to dragging and arrow keys, with no hint on the title screen. Vertical drag and arrow controls follow the requested direction; the controls also work on the main menu backdrop. Settings is also available here. The JoelTech wordmark sits at the lower right of the interface. The JT monogram appears during the loading sequence.
- **Main menu.** The camera changes angle and approaches the same crystal until its facets fill the backdrop. The crystal turns very slowly while the menu is idle. The glass tiles open **Generate phase diagrams**, **Alloy studio**, and **Settings**. One disabled **Under construction** tile reserves space for future materials workflows.
- **Alloy studio.** The second menu tile opens a submenu with **Optimize alloy** and **Create alloy**. Optimize alloy opens a five-step setup preview: alloy family, composition, processing, goals and limits, and review. Create alloy shares the same families and processing controls, but uses exact addition amounts, automatically assigns the remainder to the base metal, and skips goals and limits in navigation and review. Composition places the element picker beside the amount editor on desktop. A proportional bar and a labeled balance update as exact additions change. Both paths retain wt%/at% basis selection without implicit conversion. Step changes return to the top with focus on the step heading so the shell Back button stays available; there is no intro badge or duplicate progress row. The family aside names the source; assessment limitations remain in source help and review. The shared crystal zoom introduces the page. A large tile grid shows Mg, Al, Ti, Fe, Ni, Co, Cu, Zn, Zr, Ag, W, Pt, Au, and Pb in increasing atomic-weight order, followed by an Other tile with a + symbol. Ni precedes Co by atomic weight. The compact family buttons use beveled square symbols with glass lighting. Al, Fe, and Ni glow and have available setup previews; other families and Other are gray and disabled. Fe has a subtle red tint and Ni a subtle green tint. The grid reflows without horizontal scrolling on mobile. MatCalc sources and named mobility companions ship in the data folder and are available to the processing simulation by default; their assessment coverage is not automatically established. The optimizer entry retains the shared crystal zoom but never draws the Ti/O lattice. No atoms appear before family selection. Selecting Al lights up the atoms in a fixed FCC aluminum reference lattice one by one (instant with reduced motion) with nearest-neighbor links, using the same depth-layer renderer and settled high-DPI cache as the rutile scene. The conventional cell has corner and face-center sites. Its spacing and orientation are for display; it does not represent a calculated alloy state, and adding elements does not relabel the FCC Al sites. Opening Composition widens the workspace on desktop. The lattice smoothly magnifies by 30% as it dims to 16% opacity. Later steps retain this view; returning to the family step restores the original framing. Reduced motion applies the framing immediately, and magnified caches retain native pixel density. Leaving the optimizer preserves the selected reference lattice during the return transition.
- **Settings background.** The camera accelerates into a facet as the TiO₂ lattice comes into view. The two scenes overlap during the dive. The lens widens during the move and eases into the final field of view. Near, middle, and far atoms scale at different rates to give the lattice depth. The close framing shows fewer atoms, with space between the red O and blue Ti spheres. Each sphere has a luminous rim, soft bloom, and reflections tinted by nearby bonded neighbours. The lattice holds its orientation after the camera arrives, and atoms and bonds fade into black at the sides and in the distance. The browser caches each depth layer beyond the viewport at its widest point and renders the gem at a lower resolution during the move. When the GPU is busy, the next frame uses the current camera pose and draws the gem and lattice together. The scene uses a repeated tetragonal unit cell and simplified bonds. [Rutile crystallographic coordinates](https://github.com/cryos/avogadro/blob/master/crystals/oxides/TiO2-Rutile.cif)
- **Settings.** Natural rutile is the default: a single irregular, oblong red crystal with steady reflections and no prismatic rays. The selection applies when returning to the title or menu and on subsequent launches at the same app address. **Reduce motion** also persists there and skips the logo intro, holds the camera still, and suppresses rays and transitions. The app honors the operating system’s reduced-motion preference.
- **Materials Project settings.** Settings includes a password field, Save key, Clear session key, and a status identifying session or server environment configuration. A saved session key stays in server memory until restart, overrides any environment key, and is cleared from the field after submission. It is never saved in browser storage or run files. Clearing it restores any environment key. Failed saves keep the previous configuration and show an error. Key format is checked at save; authentication occurs during reference lookup. Appearance and integration controls sit side by side on desktop and stack on narrow screens.
- **Phase diagrams.** The shared entry zoom moves into the crystal and settles onto black, without a Ti/O lattice. The desktop workspace uses a compact header and the full window width. The center plot fits the available viewport height on typical desktop displays, and completed sidebars end after their content. Start with the setup panel filling the workspace: upload one thermodynamic file and choose two elements. Temperature in K, pressure in Pa, quality, and boundary tolerance are under **Calculation settings**, with Detailed selected by default. Creating a diagram reveals a second panel from the right, splitting the full workspace equally. It reports the current stage, boundary point count or independent check rows, and elapsed time. The progress bar sits directly under the current stage heading. Continuation has indeterminate progress; mapping batches and checks report counted progress. There is no pulsing calculation banner. Real partial boundaries and completed sample rows appear as they become available, labeled as an incomplete preview. On completion the panel becomes the diagram and the inspector enters on the right. Narrow screens stack the panels, and reduced motion suppresses transitions. Cancellation or failure preserves an explicitly incomplete preview without enabling export. The workspace keeps job state when returning to the menu.
- **Diagram inspection.** Solids use vibrant colors and liquid is always black; mixed fields blend by molar phase amounts. Phase labels use compact text with a contrast halo. Labels fit inside their phase field where possible. Narrow fields use short nearby leaders with anchor dots, with the right margin available for labels that cannot fit locally. Drag a label to move it; keyboard focus plus arrow keys moves it too, with Shift for larger steps. The anchor remains fixed at its phase location. Reset label positions restores automatic placement. Positions last for the current run and do not change exported scientific data. Red crosses identify independent sample disagreements outside the boundary tolerance (0.25% of axis span by default). Ignored differences remain inspectable. The sample count and overlay toggle sit together under Independent checks, followed by readable counted status badges. The red-cross explanation appears only when disagreements exist. Beveled phase-color chips use black or white text chosen for contrast. New uploads clear the previous result and legend; previews display only their current phases while retaining assigned colors within a run. The sample overlay, keyboard inspection, and textual check summary provide alternatives to color alone. Hover or tap to inspect, use arrow keys to move when the plot has focus, and Enter or Escape to pin or unpin. Cursor values derived from boundary interpolation are identified separately from nearest-sample results. JSON exports preserve boundaries, checks, settings, warnings, and provenance.

Use **Back** to move from a submenu to its entry screen. Escape also goes back when focus is outside form fields and the plot. The menu and workspace reflow on narrow screens.

